<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/bar-harbor.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/bar-harbor-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/bar-harbor.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/towns-we-cover/">Towns we cover</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Bar Harbor</span></nav><p class="kicker">Down East &amp; Acadia · Hancock County</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Bar Harbor, Maine</h1><p class="lede">Bar Harbor’s summer cottages, inns and seasonal homes face surge, Down East wind and winter freeze-ups while owners are away. We review carrier estimates and act as appraiser when the amount is in dispute.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="spec"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Hancock County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Down East &amp; Acadia</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="snap"><h2>Bar Harbor at a glance</h2><dl>
<div><dt>County</dt><dd>Hancock County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Down East &amp; Acadia</dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Neighborhoods and villages</dt><dd><ul class="chips"><li>Downtown</li><li>West Street</li><li>Hulls Cove</li><li>Town Hill</li><li>Salisbury Cove</li><li>Otter Creek</li><li>Hadley Point</li></ul></dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Housing mix</dt><dd>Shingle-style cottages and inns from the summer-colony era, wood-frame homes and small hotels downtown, year-round houses in Town Hill and Hulls Cove, and many seasonal homes and rentals left vacant through the winter.</dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Common claim risks</dt><dd><ul class="chips coral"><li>Shorefront surge</li><li>Post-tropical wind</li><li>Seasonal freeze-ups</li><li>Fallen spruce</li></ul></dd></div></dl>
<h3>Storms on record</h3><ol class="mini-storms"><li><b>Jan 2024</b><span><strong>Back-to-back coastal storms.</strong> Water topped the town pier, tore a hole in the Bar Harbor Inn pier and damaged the Shore Path.</span></li><li><b>Sep 2023</b><span><strong>Post-tropical Storm Lee.</strong> Strong winds downed trees and lines Down East, leaving much of Mount Desert Island without power.</span></li><li><b>Oct 1947</b><span><strong>Great Fire of 1947.</strong> Wildfire burned much of eastern Mount Desert Island, destroying many of Bar Harbor’s grand cottages.</span></li></ol></div>
<h2>Seasonal property, year-round risk</h2>
<p>A large share of Bar Harbor’s buildings sit empty from late fall to spring. Shingle-style cottages, inns, small hotels and vacation rentals close up when the cruise ships and park visitors leave, and that is when Hancock County’s weather does its work. Pipes freeze, roofs leak under snow and ice, and nobody sees the damage until weeks later. An insurance appraiser in Bar Harbor deals with timing questions as often as pricing ones.</p>
<p>Vacancy and unoccupancy terms in your policy can limit or exclude some of these losses, particularly freezing if heat was not maintained. Our <a href="/claim-math/seasonal-camp-vacancy-check/">seasonal camp and second-home vacancy check</a> helps you see how your policy terms may apply before you file.</p>
<h2>Storms Down East</h2>
<p>The January 10 and 13, 2024 storms put a foot or two of water over the town pier, tore a hole in the Bar Harbor Inn pier, and damaged the Shore Path that runs along the water. In September 2023, post-tropical storm Lee brought strong winds Down East, bringing down spruce and fir across Mount Desert Island and knocking out power to much of the island.</p>
<p>Surge and wave damage are flood, generally excluded under homeowners and commercial property policies. Wind damage to roofs, siding and windows from those same storms is usually a property claim. The distinction matters most for waterfront owners along the Shore Path, Hulls Cove and Hadley Point.</p>
<h2>Building after 1947</h2>
<p>The Great Fire of October 1947 burned much of eastern Mount Desert Island, including many of the grand summer cottages along Bar Harbor’s shore. What survived, and what was rebuilt, gives the town an unusual mix of old shingle-style buildings and mid-century construction. For fire, smoke and puffback losses, see our <a href="/appraisal-work/fire-smoke-puffback-appraisal/">fire, smoke and puffback appraisal</a> page.</p>
<h2>Pricing realities on the island</h2>
<ul>
<li>Short construction season, with contractors booked solid before and during tourist months.</li>
<li>Travel time and lodging for off-island trades.</li>
<li>Cedar shingle roofs and siding on older cottages that are hard to match.</li>
<li>Lost business income for inns and rentals, where the policy covers it.</li>
<li>Winter work on exposed sites in Salisbury Cove, Town Hill and Otter Creek.</li>
</ul>
<p>Downtown and West Street hold many older wood-frame inns and homes with tight lot lines, where a fire or a fallen tree can affect neighbors. Repairs on narrow streets during tourist season can mean limited hours, parking and staging problems, and permit timing. Year-round homes in Town Hill and Hulls Cove face more familiar Maine losses: ice dams, frozen pipes and wind damage to roofs and siding.</p>
<p>A Bar Harbor insurance appraiser should account for all of these when setting the amount of loss. Appraisal does not decide coverage questions like vacancy or flood exclusions; for those, and for policy deadlines, consult a Maine attorney.</p>

<h2>Appraisal work for Bar Harbor properties</h2>
<p>Every Maine Insurance Appraiser service is available in Bar Harbor and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/appraisal-work/homeowner-dispute-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/noreaster-hurricane-appraisal/">Nor’easter &amp; Hurricane Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/water-damage-loss-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/ice-dam-snow-load-appraisal/">Ice Dam &amp; Snow Load Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/fallen-tree-limb-appraisal/">Fallen Tree Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/carrier-estimate-line-review/">Insurance Estimate Review</a></li></ul>
<h2>Bar Harbor questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My Bar Harbor rental froze while closed for winter. Is that covered?</summary><p>It depends on your policy’s vacancy, unoccupancy and heat maintenance terms. Some policies limit freezing losses if heat was not kept on or water was not shut off, so read those sections first.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal include lost income for an inn damaged in a storm?</summary><p>If your policy includes business income coverage and the carrier accepts the claim, disputes over the amount of lost income can often go to appraisal. Check your policy’s appraisal clause.</p></details><details><summary>Does Down East repair pricing really differ from southern Maine?</summary><p>Often yes. Fewer contractors, off-island travel and a short building season can raise costs, and a fair estimate should reflect local conditions.</p></details></div>
